<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Toyotomi regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1582 CE – 1600 CE · capital Osaka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The government of Toyotomi Hideyoshi, the commoner-born warlord who completed the unification of Japan and invaded Korea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hideyoshi, a foot soldier's son who rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga's</a> service, avenged Nobunaga by defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Yamazaki</a> in 1582 and then beat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Shizugatake</a> in 1583. He began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> castle as his base, became imperial regent (kampaku) in 1585 and received the new surname <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> in 1586.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hideyoshi subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and finally the Later Hojo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> in 1590, unifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He confiscated weapons from farmers in the sword hunt of 1588, surveyed land nationwide and froze the separation of warriors and peasants. He expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries in 1587 and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sen-no-rikyu">Sen no Rikyu</a>. His invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1592 and 1597 ended in failure.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On Hideyoshi's death in 1598 a council of five elders governed for his young son Hideyori. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> power, though Hideyori held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> until its fall in 1615.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/toyotomi-regime.jpg" alt="Toyotomi regime" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: file created on Adobe Illustrator and Photoshop by: Zagyoso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goshichi_no_kiri_inverted.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1582 CE – 1600 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Osaka</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toyotomi Hideyoshi, Toyotomi Hidetsugu, Toyotomi Hideyori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Toyotomi administration, Toyotomi clan, Hideyoshi's regime</dd></dl></aside></div>
